{"name":"Rogue Cowboy","hex":"#ba9e88","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"rogue","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A scoundrel, rascal or unprincipled, deceitful, and unreliable person.","A mischievous scamp."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Vicious and solitary.","Large, destructive and unpredictable."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/rogue"},{"term":"cowboy","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A man who tends free-range cattle, especially in the American West.","A man who identifies with cowboy culture, including wearing a cowboy hat and being a fan of country and western music."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To work as a cowboy, herding cattle."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/cowboy"}],"entries":[{"title":"Cowboy","blocks":[{"text":"A cowboy is an animal herder who tends cattle on ranches in North America, traditionally on horseback, and often performs many other ranch-related tasks. The historic American cowboy of the late 19th century arose from the vaquero traditions of northern Mexico, which trace their roots back to Spanish settlers from Andalusia and became a figure of special significance and legend. A subtype, called a wrangler, specifically tends the horses used to work cattle. In addition to ranch work, some cowboys work for or participate in rodeos. Cowgirls, first defined as such in the late 19th century, had a less-well documented historical role, but in the modern world work at identical tasks and have obtained considerable respect for their achievements. Cattle handlers in many other parts of the world, particularly South America and Australia, perform work similar to the cowboy.\nThe cowboy has deep historic roots tracing back to Spain and the earliest European settlers of the Americas. Over the centuries, differences in terrain and climate, and the influence of cattle-handling traditions from multiple cultures, created several distinct styles of equipment, clothing and animal handling. Equipment and techniques have adapted over time, though many classic traditions are preserved."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d1/HerdQuit.jpg/330px-HerdQuit.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cowboy"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/73/The_Cow_Boy_1888.jpg/500px-The_Cow_Boy_1888.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/73/The_Cow_Boy_1888.jpg/1280px-The_Cow_Boy_1888.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/73/The_Cow_Boy_1888.jpg/500px-The_Cow_Boy_1888.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"American cowboy, 1887","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AThe_Cow_Boy_1888.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/87/King_of_the_Plains.jpg/500px-King_of_the_Plains.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/87/King_of_the_Plains.jpg/500px-King_of_the_Plains.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"\"King of the Plains\" postcard, 1898–1924","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AKing_of_the_Plains.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d8/Dragon_de_cuera.jpg/500px-Dragon_de_cuera.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d8/Dragon_de_cuera.jpg/1280px-Dragon_de_cuera.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d8/Dragon_de_cuera.jpg/500px-Dragon_de_cuera.jpg?utm_source=en.wikiped